 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Complexity:** The selection logic is $O(1)$ per instance, involving simple distance comparisons. The primary cost is the memory overhead of storing multiple mesh variants per instance type and the CPU cost of generating instances that are subsequently culled [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Reduces vertex count for distant objects without requiring complex geometry simplification algorithms at runtime. Allows for "impostor" techniques (e.g., using simple quads for distant trees) [S1]. Avoids the scene graph overhead of managing separate objects for each LOD level [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Limited to 4 LOD levels (LOD0-LOD3) per instance type [S1]. Switching meshes dynamically may incur performance penalties [S1]. Does not reduce the cost of instance generation, only rendering [S1].
-*   **When to use:** When rendering large numbers of identical objects (e.g., forests) where distant objects can be represented by lower-poly meshes or impostors [S1]. When the primary volume LOD is insufficient for mesh-level detail control [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of visible instances, as each instance requires a distance calculation and a constant-time lookup (up to 4 comparisons) to select the mesh [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N \times M)$ where $M$ is the number of LOD levels per instance, as all LOD meshes must be loaded/available in memory for the instances [G].
+*   **Strengths:** Reduces vertex count significantly for distant objects; allows use of 2D impostors for extreme distances; decouples mesh detail from terrain resolution [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Only applies to `VoxelInstanceLibraryMultiMeshItem` instances; does not reduce memory usage of the instances themselves, only rendering cost; dynamic mesh switching may incur engine-specific performance penalties [S1].
+*   **When to use:** When rendering large numbers of distinct objects (e.g., trees, buildings) where geometric detail is not needed at a distance.
+*   **When not to use:** When memory bandwidth is the primary bottleneck (since all LODs are loaded) or when using engines that handle mesh LOD automatically (e.g., Godot 4+) [S1].
 
 ## Background
-This system was introduced in the Voxel Tools addon for Godot 4 to address the lack of automatic mesh LOD in Godot 3 [S1]. It serves as an alternative to using Visibility Ranges with MultiMesh, which requires duplicating data and increasing the number of scene objects to process [S1]. Godot 4 later added automatic mesh LOD, but this system remains for users requiring fine-grained control over mesh and material assignment per LOD level [S1].
+Mesh Level of Detail in this context originates from the Voxel Tools plugin for the Godot game engine [S1]. It was introduced to address the lack of automatic mesh LOD in Godot 3, providing a manual override for `VoxelInstanceLibraryMultiMeshItem` to control mesh and material usage per LOD level [S1]. Godot 4 later added automatic mesh LOD, making this system less critical but still useful for fine-grained control [S1].
 
